---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/biology/foundational/photosynthesis-respiration">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Every calorie you have ever burned was once sunlight. Plants — and algae, and some bacteria —
    run the only large-scale process on Earth that converts light into storable chemical energy:
    <strong>photosynthesis</strong>, inside organelles called chloroplasts. Everything else, from
    fungi to philosophers, lives by <strong>respiring</strong> that stored energy in their
    mitochondria. The two processes are a loop with the Sun at the top: photosynthesis builds
    sugar out of thin air and water, respiration burns the sugar back to air and water, and the
    difference is paid for by photons.
  </p>
  <p>
    People worked this out by surprisingly simple experiments. In the 1640s Jan Baptista van
    Helmont grew a willow in 91 kg of dried soil for five years, watering it only; the tree gained
    74 kg while the soil lost almost nothing — the first hint that a plant's mass does
    <em>not</em> come from the soil (he guessed water; the other partner, carbon dioxide, was
    still unknown). In 1771 Joseph Priestley sealed a mouse and a lit candle in a jar: the candle
    went out, the mouse would have died, but a sprig of mint "restored the air". Within a decade
    Jan Ingenhousz showed the restoration needed <em>light</em>. The pieces assembled into the
    equation: light + water + carbon dioxide → sugar + oxygen.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the light response — more light helps, until the machinery itself is the bottleneck" widget="plotter" config={lightCfg} />

  <p>
    The curve above is the most useful graph in plant science, and it teaches the idea of
    <strong>limiting factors</strong>: whatever resource is scarcest sets the rate. In dim light,
    photons are the bottleneck, so the rate climbs steeply with every extra lumen. Then it
    flattens — light is no longer the limit; the supply of carbon dioxide, the speed of the
    enzymes, or the temperature is. Raising rₘₐₓ lifts the plateau; raising k shifts where it
    bends. Real leaves sit near this saturation point in full sun — which is why commercial
    greenhouse growers pump in extra CO₂ (about 0.1%, versus 0.04% outdoors) and why shade plants
    differ from sun plants mainly in their k. One experiment: an aquatic plant in a beaker,
    counting oxygen bubbles per minute, doubles its bubble count as the lamp moves closer — then
    stops responding no matter how bright it gets. The graph, done empirically.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="the two equations of life, step by step" widget="equationStep" config={eqCfg} />

  <p>
    Read the two equations together and notice they are near-mirror images — but with completely
    different machinery. Photosynthesis is an uphill build (energy goes <em>in</em>, from light);
    respiration is a downhill burn (energy comes <em>out</em>, as ATP). No single factory could
    run both in one room, so the cell splits them: photosynthesis in chloroplasts, respiration in
    mitochondria, ATP shuttling between like money moving between accounts.
  </p>

  <h2>Inside the leaf: structure fitted to the job</h2>
  <p>
    A leaf is a photochemical factory arranged in layers, and each layer is optimised for one
    constraint. The top wears a transparent, waxy <strong>cuticle</strong> to stop water
    escaping while letting light through. Beneath it sits the <strong>palisade
    mesophyll</strong>: upright, tightly packed cells crammed with chloroplasts, positioned where
    the light is brightest. Below that, the <strong>spongy mesophyll</strong> is loose, with air
    spaces so carbon dioxide can diffuse freely to every photosynthesising cell. The underside's
    <strong>lower epidermis</strong> is pierced by <strong>stomata</strong> — pores that are the
    leaf's lungs. Each stoma is flanked by two curved <strong>guard cells</strong> that swell
    with water to open the pore and sag to close it: open, the leaf can breathe in CO₂; closed,
    it stops losing water in a drought. A typical leaf carries 100 or more stomata per square
    millimetre of underside — a deliberate trade of water for carbon.
  </p>
  <p>
    The veins, finally, are the logistics. <strong>Xylem</strong> vessels carry water up from
    the roots (the raw material for photosynthesis, and the thing keeping cells turgid);
    <strong>phloem</strong> ships the finished sucrose away to growing shoots, ripening fruit
    and underground storage. Every layer answers the same three demands — light in, CO₂ in,
    water managed — and a structure like the palisade layer, or the stoma-guard pair, is the
    exam-board's favourite proof that form follows function.
  </p>

  <h2>How photosynthesis actually works</h2>
  <p>
    In words: <code>carbon dioxide + water → glucose + oxygen</code>, with light and
    chlorophyll doing the driving. In balanced symbols: 6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂. It runs in
    two stages. In the <strong>light-dependent stage</strong>, chlorophyll in the thylakoid
    membranes absorbs photons and the energy rips water molecules apart — the oxygen you are
    breathing right now is this step's waste product. The captured energy is banked as ATP and a
    hydrogen-carrier called NADPH. In the <strong>light-independent stage</strong> (the Calvin
    cycle, in the fluid stroma), that banked energy powers the fixing of CO₂ onto a five-carbon
    sugar, which is rebuilt and stripped of carbon atoms until glucose emerges. Crucially, the
    second stage needs the first stage's products, but not the light itself.
  </p>
  <p>
    Temperature plays the role students least expect: photosynthesis is enzyme chemistry, so
    its rate rises with warmth to an optimum (around 25–35 °C for most crops) and then
    <em>collapses</em> as the enzymes denature. Light, CO₂ and temperature — three dials, any
    one of which can be the limiting factor at any moment, and the art of the greenhouse is
    knowing which dial is currently the binding one. One curiosity explains the colour of
    everything green: chlorophyll absorbs red and blue light hardest and reflects green, so
    the garden's colour is literally sunlight's leftovers.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> plants do not respire "instead of" photosynthesising, and
  their mass does not come from the soil. Every living plant cell respires all day and all
  night; photosynthesis simply out-earns that spending in daylight. And a tree's bulk is built
  overwhelmingly from carbon pulled out of the air as CO₂ (plus water), not from dirt — van
  Helmont's willow proved the soil contributes almost nothing, even though he could not yet
  explain where the carbon came from.</p>

  <h2>Respiration: cashing in the sugar</h2>
  <p>
    Respiration is <em>not</em> breathing — breathing merely delivers oxygen; respiration is the
    chemical process in every one of your cells that releases energy from food. In words:
    <code>glucose + oxygen → carbon dioxide + water + energy</code>. Because it needs oxygen it
    is called <strong>aerobic respiration</strong>, and because it runs at about 37 °C inside
    thousands of gentle enzyme steps it is combustion with the fire removed — same overall
    reaction as burning sugar in a flame, but the energy is captured in small, spendable
    increments as ATP instead of being wasted as one burst of heat and light.
  </p>
  <p>
    What is the energy <em>for</em>? Building molecules (proteins, new membranes), muscle
    contraction, active transport pumping ions across membranes, nerve signalling, and cell
    division. Your brain, about 2% of your body weight, respires roughly 20% of your resting
    energy. ATP is the interface: think of photosynthesis as income, respiration as salary day,
    and ATP as the currency every single purchase is made in — your body recycles roughly its
    own weight of ATP molecules every day, recharging and spending them in continuous cycles.
  </p>

  <h2>Without oxygen: anaerobic respiration</h2>
  <p>
    Sprint for the bus and your muscles outrun your lungs: oxygen delivery cannot match demand.
    Muscle cells switch to <strong>anaerobic respiration</strong>: <code>glucose → lactic acid +
    energy</code>. It is an emergency measure for two reasons — it releases far less energy per
    glucose (about 2 ATP, versus roughly 30 aerobically), and the lactic acid accumulates,
    lowering pH and contributing to the ache in your thighs. Worse, the lactic acid must later
    be broken down <em>aerobically</em>, mostly in the liver — which is why you keep panting
    hard for minutes after stopping. That repayment is the <strong>oxygen debt</strong>: your
    elevated breathing after the race is not recovery theatre, it is the bill.
  </p>
  <p>
    Other organisms run the anaerobic route differently, and civilisation runs on the
    difference. Yeast performs <code>glucose → ethanol + carbon dioxide + energy</code> —
    fermentation — which gives beer and wine their alcohol and bread its rising (the CO₂ puffs
    the dough; the ethanol evaporates in baking). Bacteria fermenting milk sugar produce the
    lactic acid that turns milk into yogurt and cheese. Any deep, oxygen-free mud, cow rumen or
    landfill produces methane for the same reason. One chemistry, three industries.
  </p>

  <h2>Seeing photosynthesis happen: the classic experiments</h2>
  <p>
    Photosynthesis is invisible, so biologists designed tests that make it legible. The
    classic is the <strong>starch test</strong>: boil a leaf in ethanol to strip its
    chlorophyll, rinse, then flood it with iodine — blue-black means starch stored, which is
    the leaf's banked photosynthesis (glucose is converted to insoluble starch for storage).
    Destarch a plant in the dark for 48 hours first, then the iodine becomes an honest meter:
    anything that turns black afterwards was <em>made</em> during the experiment. Cover part
    of a leaf with foil and only the uncovered half goes black — light is necessary. A leaf
    from a plant in a sealed soda-lime jar (which scrubs CO₂ from the air) stays amber —
    carbon dioxide is necessary. And a variegated leaf (white patches, no chlorophyll) goes
    black only where it was green — chlorophyll is necessary. Three controls, three
    conclusions: this is the experimental logic the syllabus wants you to be able to design,
    not just recite. Counting oxygen bubbles from pondweed, as in fig. 1's thought
    experiment, is the same idea measuring <em>rate</em> rather than presence.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The two processes also tangle at dusk. As light fades, a plant's photosynthesis rate slides
    down toward its respiration rate; the <strong>compensation point</strong> is where the two
    are exactly equal — the leaf earns what it spends. Below it (night, deep shade), the plant
    is a net consumer of oxygen, which is why keeping plants in a sealed bedroom is
    undramatically harmless: one houseplant's night-time respiration is trivial beside yours.
    Scale the loop up, though, and it runs the planet: land plants and ocean plankton fix
    roughly 120 billion tonnes of carbon into sugar every year, and about 2.4 billion years ago
    their cyanobacterial ancestors pumped so much oxygen into the sky (the Great Oxidation
    Event) that they poisoned most anaerobic life — and rusted the oceans' iron into the banded
    iron formations we now mine. The oxygen in your next breath is, quite literally, photosynthesis
    waste with two billion years of provenance.
  </p>
  <p>
    There are refinements to the basic design, too. Photosynthesis's CO₂-grabbing enzyme works
    inefficiently when it is hot and CO₂ is scarce, accidentally grabbing oxygen instead —
    <em>photorespiration</em>, a costly mistake. Maize, sugarcane and many grasses pre-concentrate
    CO₂ in special cells first (the C₄ pathway); desert cacti go further, opening their stomata
    only at night and storing the CO₂ as an acid until daylight (CAM photosynthesis). And at the
    frontier, researchers are re-engineering photosynthesis itself: synthetic biologists have
    rebuilt the Calvin cycle's bottleneck enzymes in the lab and trialled C₄-style rice, hoping
    to push yields up in a warming, CO₂-rich century. The two equations in fig. 2 look settled;
    the machinery inside them is still being tuned — by evolution, and now by us.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Priestley (1771) and Ingenhousz (1779) experiments; OpenStax, <em>Biology 2e</em> (Photosynthesis; Cellular Respiration); Campbell, <em>Biology</em>; Cambridge IGCSE Biology 0615 syllabus, sections 6.1–6.2 and 12.1–12.3.</p>
</TopicPage>
